      <section>
        <h2 class="text-lg sm:text-xl font-bold text-ink mb-3">2. The Reality of Sizing: Our Sizing Disclaimer</h2>
        <p>Let's talk about the elephant in the room: bra sizing is incredibly inconsistent. If you've ever found that you're a 34C in one brand, a 32D in another, and a 36B in a third, you are not alone, and you are not imagining things. The apparel industry, particularly when it comes to intimates, does not have a universal, globally enforced standard for measurements.</p>
        <p>Because of this, the sizing recommendations provided by BraSizeChecker.com are intended strictly for guidance and educational purposes. We have poured our hearts, souls, and countless hours of research into developing algorithms and formulas that are as accurate as possible. However, they are still mathematical estimations based on the measurements you provide. They cannot account for every unique nuance of the human body.</p>
        <p>Fit is deeply personal and subjective. It can vary wildly based on a multitude of factors, including but not limited to:</p>
        <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-2 mt-2">
            <li><strong>Brand Variability:</strong> Different manufacturers use different fit models and grading systems. A "Large" or a "36DD" means entirely different things depending on whose label is stitched inside.</li>
            <li><strong>Style and Cut:</strong> A balconette, a plunge, a full-coverage, and a bralette will all fit differently, even if they are marked as the exact same size by the exact same brand. The architecture of the garment matters.</li>
            <li><strong>Fabric Composition:</strong> Stretchy lace behaves differently than rigid satin. A band made of firm elastic will feel tighter than a band made of modal blend, affecting the size you might need.</li>
            <li><strong>Breast Shape and Tissue Distribution:</strong> Two people with the exact same underbust and overbust measurements can have vastly different breast shapes (e.g., shallow, projected, full-on-top, full-on-bottom). Shape dictates fit just as much as volume does.</li>
            <li><strong>Personal Preference:</strong> Some people prefer a very snug, locked-in feel, while others prioritize a looser, relaxed fit. What feels "perfect" to one person might feel intolerable to another.</li>
            <li><strong>Measurement Accuracy:</strong> The numbers we calculate are only as accurate as the measurements you input. Taking measurements over thick clothing, using a stretched-out tape measure, or measuring at different times of the month can skew the results.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Therefore, we cannot and do not guarantee a perfect, magical fit straight out of the box based solely on our recommendations. Consider our calculator's result as your starting point—a well-informed educated guess that gives you a baseline to work from when you hit the store or shop online. We strongly encourage you to try on multiple sizes around your recommended size, experiment with different styles, and ultimately trust your own body and comfort above any number a calculator gives you.</p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="text-lg sm:text-xl font-bold text-ink mb-3">3. Crucial Medical Disclaimer: We Are Not Doctors</h2>
        <p>This is a very important section. Please read it carefully. <strong>BraSizeChecker.com does not offer, provide, or substitute for professional medical advice, diagnosis, or treatment.</strong></p>
        <p>We are bra fitting enthusiasts, researchers, and advocates for comfort. We are not physicians, nurses, physical therapists, chiropractors, dermatologists, or medical professionals of any kind. All the content on our site—including text, graphics, calculator outputs, sizing guides, and articles—is for general informational and educational purposes only.</p>
        <p>An ill-fitting bra can certainly cause physical discomfort, but it is critical to distinguish between normal clothing discomfort and potential medical issues. Here are examples of why a bra fitting or sizing recommendation is never medical advice:</p>
        <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-2 mt-2">
            <li><strong>Breast Pain (Mastalgia):</strong> While a too-tight bra can cause aching, breast pain can also be a symptom of hormonal changes, cysts, infections, or other underlying medical conditions. If you are experiencing persistent, severe, or new breast pain, a calculator cannot diagnose it. You must consult a doctor.</li>
            <li><strong>Back, Neck, and Shoulder Pain:</strong> Carrying heavy breast tissue in an unsupportive bra can lead to strain. However, chronic back or neck pain could also be related to spinal issues, muscular disorders, or posture problems that require physical therapy or medical intervention, not just a new bra size.</li>
            <li><strong>Skin Issues and Dermatological Concerns:</strong> A tight band rubbing against your skin might cause chafing or mild irritation. But rashes, persistent redness, sores, changes in skin texture (like dimpling), or unusual discharge are medical symptoms. A bra fitting guide cannot address skin infections, allergic reactions to fabrics, or signs of breast disease.</li>
            <li><strong>Changes in Breast Size or Shape:</strong> Breasts naturally fluctuate in size due to weight changes, menstrual cycles, pregnancy, and aging. However, sudden, unexplained, or asymmetrical changes in size, shape, or density require professional medical evaluation to rule out serious health concerns.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>We want you to be comfortable, but more importantly, we want you to be healthy. If you are experiencing pain, severe discomfort, unusual skin reactions, or any symptoms that concern you, <strong>do not rely on a bra calculator to solve the problem.</strong> Please, please seek the advice of your physician or other qualified healthcare provider with any questions you may have regarding a medical condition. Never disregard professional medical advice or delay in seeking it because of something you have read on BraSizeChecker.com.</p>
      </section>

